return [====[ Value is a comparison score, not cash in hand.</p><p>Properties can affect abilities, saves, damage, speed, Dodge, Block, regeneration, Push, map cost, breadcrumbs, summons, elemental wards, weaknesses, and hit riders. Conditional damage bonuses say when they work: injured, almost stationary, or highly charged with Magic. The equipment-property reference lists every current property.</p><p>These rarity percentages describe the underlying random roll. Named equipment archetypes can impose a higher minimum rarity. Their stable name identifies a guaranteed combination of effects and a deliberate drawback; extra rolls still vary within the same bounded value budget.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 33</div></article><article class="page" id="big-loot" data-title="Build a different rescue"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Build a different rescue</h2><table><tr><td>Surveyor’s Lantern</td><td>Longer breadcrumbs and cheaper mapping; weaker physical protection.</td></tr><tr><td>Infirmary Coat</td><td>Higher regeneration ceiling and faster recovery; slower movement.</td></tr><tr><td>Measured Retreat</td><td>Quickstep plus stationary physical offense; weaker protection.</td></tr><tr><td>Blood Price</td><td>Wounded physical damage plus Bleeding attempts; stronger incoming physical damage.</td></tr><tr><td>Bulkhead Buckler</td><td>Block plus resistance to Push; slower movement.</td></tr><tr><td>Glass Command</td><td>More summon capacity and Magic damage; weaker protection.</td></tr></table><p>The prison now holds 113 named equipment archetypes alongside the existing equipment, special items and consumables: 158 meaningful identities in all. Headwear, body armor, legwear, boots, rings, gloves, shields and all seven weapon types have distinct alternatives. Their names describe repeatable mechanical roles, not just a new coat of paint.</p><p>Loot develops local themes: scavenged, military, medical, occult, elemental, industrial, mobility, defensive, unstable and expedition gear. The director remembers recent items, families, slots and effects, so repeated finds become less likely while unseen choices gain weight. A theme is a tendency, never a promise of a particular item.</p><p>Optional branches can reward a deeper detour, a dead end or a vertical route. Some sectors are leaner in equipment; others favor caches. Baseline healing and ammunition remain separate from build-defining equipment, and no rare item is required to finish a dungeon.</p><p>Read both the guaranteed effects and the drawback. Two pieces can support the same tactic without a set bonus. Wearables work only while equipped; weapons work only while held. Shared Dodge, Block, status saves, element rules and aggregate equipment caps still apply.</p><p>Loot remains individualized. Reconnecting or inspecting an existing reward does not reroll it. The bag still holds 32 permanent records, including equipped gear. Trashing or selling a weapon cannot bring it back through an old saved weapon snapshot. Fuse and sell at Debbie under the same displayed value rules.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 34</div></article><article class="page" id="supplies" data-title="Supplies in the corridors"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Supplies in the corridors</h2><p>Ordinary drops are character-owned. A pickup you can inspect is not necessarily an item another player can claim. Walk into your eligible pickup to collect it. A full bag or full consumable stack leaves an unaccepted pickup available rather than silently destroying it.</p><p>Early dungeons offer more need-aware resource assistance: shortages and long dry spells can influence useful drops. This assistance fades through Dungeons 2–10. From Dungeon 11 onward, its pity, objective guarantees and resource-budget supplements end. Optional treasure branches remain, and equipment selection continues to use campaign history and local motifs. A nearly full bag reduces new equipment drops; a full bag redirects those opportunities toward usable health or ammunition, otherwise nothing drops.</p><p>Normal ammo pickups add up to 6 Pistol rounds, 3 Shotgun shells, 9 SMG rounds, 6 Pulse Rifle rounds, or 2 Revolver rounds, limited by room. The carried caps still include the magazine. Equipment drops, consumables, and temporary ammunition are ordinary run inventory; they are not persistent DFTs.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 35</div></article><article class="page" id="bottles" data-title="A bottle for a friend"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A bottle for a friend</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>Healing Potions, Stink Bombs, Summon Cards and Feathers of Resurrection each stack up to three. Equip a stack in the Throwable slot, then select it as your held weapon. Its controls appear on screen.</p><p>Healing Potion: right mouse drinks it to restore up to 25 HP. At full ordinary HP, drinking is rejected without consuming one. Left mouse throws it; the first living allied Hero hit receives the healing. There is no healing splash. A missed throw still spends the bottle.</p><p>Stink Bomb: left mouse throws a five-second poison cloud with a 96-unit radius. Enemies entering make the normal Constitution save against Poisoned. You cannot drink a Stink Bomb.</p><p>Summon Card: choose another deployed Hero. Left mouse brings them to a safe adjacent square; right mouse brings them to your square or its nearest safe equivalent. Closed gates still apply. A cancelled or failed selection spends nothing.</p><p>Feather of Resurrection: either mouse button restores the oldest eligible zero-life Hero in the Hero Queue with exactly one life, preserving their character and surviving inventory. Active Soldiers and Soldier respawn waits are excluded; returning to the Hero Queue restores eligibility. Full Hero slots defer admission. No eligible Hero means no Feather spent.</p><p>These bottles, cards and Feathers spend no Magic. Holding one replaces spell controls; switch weapons before trying to cast a spell.</p><p>Healing Potions are remedies: they also clear every active negative condition, even at full HP. Magic bombs cover Physical, RAW, all six elements and every condition. They share normal throwable slots and saves; their 144-unit explosion cannot hit through solid walls or floors.</p><blockquote>“The red one is medicine. Please read the label.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 36</div></article><article class="page" id="moves" data-title="Equipment with a trick"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Equipment with a trick</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 100%"></div><table><tr><td>Quickstep · boots</td><td>↑ ↑ ↑ · 10 Magic · 2-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Rebuff · ring</td><td>← ↓ → · 20 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Psychic Crush · Crown of Psychic Crushing</td><td>↓ ↑ ↓ · 18 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>Quickstep dashes forward up to 90 units over 0.2 seconds; walls stop it. Ordinary voluntary-speed Dodge rules apply. It is not permission to cross a locked gate.</p><p>Rebuff sends a one-cell burst of 1d6 untyped Magic, followed by a 96-unit Push attempt against surviving damaged enemies.</p><p>Psychic Crush targets the nearest visible enemy within two connected maze cells. Closed gates and solid cover block it. It deals 2d8 plus your Wisdom bonus as Magic; the enemy makes a Wisdom save for half damage. The die readout identifies the target and save. With no valid target, you spend no Magic. The Crown is at least Rare and its innate power is included in its value.</p><p>You must equip an item that grants the move. Enter its sequence with the arrow keys. The default alternate keys map Up to [, Down to comma, Left to semicolon, and Right to backslash. Rebind those mirror keys in Equipment if needed.</p><p>Pause more than 0.8 seconds between inputs and the sequence resets. An unavailable move, insufficient Magic, or an active cooldown cannot be bypassed by entering the combination faster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 37</div></article><article class="page" id="fighting-streets" data-title="Gloves of the Fighting Streets"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Gloves of the Fighting Streets</h2><table><tr><td>Ember Fist</td><td>← ↓ → · 12 Magic · 2-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Cinder Rise</td><td>→ ↓ → · 18 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>These paired gloves occupy both hands and grant both techniques while equipped. They are at least Rare; their affixes and innate techniques contribute to their value. Stowing them removes the techniques.</p><p>Ember Fist launches a straight elemental fireball up to five cells (1920 units), dealing 2d6 plus Wisdom as Magic to the first enemy hit. Solid cover stops it; there is no splash or piercing. The gloves’ affinity determines the element and its ordinary Content rider: Earth Push, Fire Immolated, Dark Poisoned, Ice Held, Light Muted or Electric Morale.</p><p>Cinder Rise strikes visible enemies in your current maze square for 2d8 plus Wisdom as Magic, using the gloves’ element. Surviving damaged enemies attempt the ordinary Immolated save. Its upward effect does not move your Hero; walls and floors block its hits.</p><p>Both use ordinary offensive Magic cost modifiers, without a Content surcharge. A committed miss still costs Magic. An invalid source, blocked launch, insufficient Magic or cooldown spends nothing. Stale fireballs disappear if you die, change roles or dungeons, or remove the source gloves; they do not refund Magic.</p><p>Rebuff uses the same sequence as Ember Fist, but its ring cannot stay equipped alongside these two-hand gloves. Read your equipped item’s details before entering the sequence.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 38</div></article><article class="page" id="invisibility-ring" data-title="Ring of Invisibility"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Ring of Invisibility</h2><table><tr><td>Veil</td><td>← ↑ ← · 20 Magic · 20-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>This single-hand ring grants up to 12 seconds of Invisibility while equipped. It is at least Rare and can occupy either hand. Paired gloves displace it. Its generated properties and innate technique both contribute to its value.</p><p>AI enemies lose you as a target while invisible. Their shots already in flight, committed area attacks, environmental hazards, and manually aimed human Soldiers can still hurt you. Sixth Sense can reveal your silhouette under its ordinary visibility rules.</p><p>Primary or secondary attack input reveals you, even if the attempt is dry or blocked. Magic on any bound button, an offensive item technique, a throw attempt, or dealing or taking HP damage also reveals you. Walking, jumping, reloading, opening menus and drinking a potion alone preserve Veil.</p><p>Removing or moving the source ring, death, a role or dungeon change, and other lifecycle transitions end Veil. It cannot refresh while active. Ending never refunds Magic or resets the cooldown. Failed activation spends nothing; Veil costs a fixed 20 Magic.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 39</div></article><article class="page" id="thunder-hat" data-title="Hat of the Thunder God"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hat of the Thunder God</h2><table><tr><td>Thunder Charge</td><td>↑ ↓ ↑ · 24 base Magic · 6-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>This Rare-or-better headwear grants Thunder Charge while equipped. Its generated properties and innate technique both contribute to its value. Stowing or replacing the Hat ends an active charge.</p><p>An electric-blue path and sound warn for a quarter-second, then you charge straight along your original aim for up to three blocks in 0.8 seconds. You cannot steer. Walls, locked gates, actors, stairs, ledges, wet or harmful spaces stop the charge. Start on a supported flat floor with at least 32 units of safe room.</p><p>The first enemy contact deals one hit of 2d8 plus Wisdom as electric Magic, with its normal Morale rider. Surviving enemies that lose HP may suffer ordinary hit-stun, subject to their existing immunities and retrigger rules. Allies stop your charge without taking damage. There is no piercing or splash.</p><p>The charge grants no invulnerability, flight or extra Dodge. It uses ordinary offensive Magic cost modifiers and reveals Veil. Failed activation is free; once committed, interruption or a miss does not refund Magic or reset the cooldown. Removal, death, role or dungeon changes, forced movement, Held or Muted cancel it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 40</div></article><article class="page" id="heavy-plumber" data-title="Boots of the Heavy Plumber"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Heavy Plumber</h2><table><tr><td>Heavy Stomp</td><td>Land from above · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>These Rare-or-better boots occupy your feet slot. Equip them before leaving solid ground, then land on an enemy from above to deal 2d6 plus Strength as physical damage. Your usual dice, defenses and damage modifiers apply. Sideways contact and landing on allies do not attack.</p><p>A valid stomp gives you a short upward rebound, even if the enemy dodges or blocks the damage. Low ceilings reduce the rebound. With insufficient clearance or unsafe floor support below, you land normally without an attack. The Boots do not grant flight, invulnerability or protection from fall damage.</p><p>You get one stomp attempt per airborne excursion, with at least 0.65 seconds between commitments. Your bounce is part of that same excursion. Land on solid, non-actor support to rearm; standing on an enemy or swapping Boots in midair will not do it.</p><p>Heavy Stomp needs no arrow sequence or Magic. Muted and holding a potion do not disable it; Held does. A committed stomp reveals Veil. Removing the Boots or changing life, role or dungeon invalidates the armed landing.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 41</div></article><article class="page" id="magic-hourglass" data-title="A little more time"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little more time</h2><table><tr><td>Magic Hourglass</td><td>LMB / RMB: USE · Adds 2d4 minutes · Stack of 3</td></tr></table><p>This rare consumable buys the whole party more time. Equip and hold it in the Throwable slot, then press either mouse button. One Hourglass is consumed; the server rolls two ordinary four-sided dice and adds their sum in minutes to the current dungeon clock. The Die Log shows both dice, and everyone&#x27;s clock updates immediately.</p><p>The result is always two through eight minutes: no exploding dice, class bonuses or Magic cost. Any Hero class can use it, with the ordinary 0.6-second item cooldown. A stack holds at most three, and each legitimate use adds its full result even above 30:00.</p><p>Use it before time runs out. At or after the deadline, TIME OVER wins; the Hourglass cannot reverse the collapse. Paused staging clocks, failed or cleared dungeons, Soldier bodies and unequipped items cannot spend it. Successful rescue resets the next dungeon to 30:00, while unused Hourglasses remain in your Hero&#x27;s inventory.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 42</div></article><article class="page" id="damsels-revenge" data-title="Damsel&#x27;s Revenge"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Damsel&#x27;s Revenge</h2><table><tr><td>Damsel&#x27;s Revenge</td><td>LMB / RMB: USE · Throwable stack of 3 · One use per fight</td></tr></table><p>This rare consumable gives the jailed Damsel a chance to fight back. Equip and hold it in the Throwable slot, enter the committed Gordon fight, and press either mouse button from the court—not the safe entrance. One unit arms her with a random Pistol, SMG or Pulse Rifle. It costs no Magic or ammunition and works for every Hero class. Invalid use spends nothing.</p><p>She stays in her jail and shoots through a visible firing slit at the real, visible Gordon. Walls and intervening bodies still block her fire; the jail remains locked and solid to Heroes. She cannot shoot fakes, turrets, teammates or Hector. Her protection does not change. She uses the gun&#x27;s basic physical damage, without your abilities, feats or equipment bonuses. Its procedural properties become available only after normal collection and equipping.</p><p>One activation serves the whole party for that encounter. Further uses cannot replace or duplicate her gun. Your death or disconnection does not disarm her; she pauses when no living Hero is in the court. Gordon&#x27;s defeat stops her combat immediately, including before Hector appears at Dungeon Level 20. Normal Jail Key and rescue requirements still apply.</p><p>After successful rescue, she drops that same gun at her feet. It belongs to the Hero who used Damsel&#x27;s Revenge. Touch the pickup or press E at her feet during the existing 20-second victory window; her own Touch/Use also forwards collection when her body covers it. Make room in your equipment bag if necessary. Collection stores the gun without equipping it or granting ammunition. There is no automatic replacement for an uncollected gun, and leaving the dungeon ends the opportunity.</p><p>Damsel&#x27;s Revenge may replace a small fraction of remaining ordinary Healing Potion loot opportunities; fixed supplies are unchanged. It does not work in staging, after Gordon&#x27;s defeat, during failure or after time runs out. Dungeon Levels 21 onward have cash instead of a jailed Damsel, so the item cannot be used there. Reset or campaign failure ends the activation without refund.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 43</div></article><article class="page" id="moon-boots" data-title="Boots of the Moon"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Moon</h2><table><tr><td>Moon Gravity</td><td>25% lower gravity · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip these Rare-or-better boots in your feet slot for higher jumps and longer airtime in the dungeon. Gravity is reduced by 25%; ordinary steering and horizontal movement remain yours to control. No arrow sequence, Magic cost or cooldown is required.</p><p>Your usual jump, Cloud Step and Wall Jump impulses still apply. Float On keeps its own controls and Magic cost. Invisible maze walls, locked gates, collisions and fall damage remain in force: these boots do not grant flight or invulnerability.</p><p>Removing or replacing the boots restores ordinary gravity, including in midair. Their effect ends on death, staging or role change and returns when the same equipped boots belong to a living, deployed Hero again. Human Soldiers cannot borrow the Hero&#x27;s equipment.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 44</div></article><article class="page" id="tanuki-ring" data-title="Tanuki’s Ring"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Tanuki’s Ring</h2><table><tr><td>Statue</td><td>Stand still for 2 seconds · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip this Rare-or-better ring in either hand and stand still on solid ground for two continuous seconds. You become stone: enemies stop seeing and targeting you, and direct hits, explosions, hazards and damaging statuses cannot take your HP while Statue remains active.</p><p>Movement immediately ends Statue, including jumps, crouching, forced propulsion, moving supports and teleportation. Attacking, casting Magic, using an item or interacting also ends it, even if the action fails. Dealing HP damage ends it too. You must stand still for another full two seconds; looking around alone is allowed.</p><p>Statue costs no Magic and has no fixed duration or cooldown. It does not cure existing conditions or stop the dungeon collapse clock. Removing or moving the ring, dying, changing role or changing dungeon cancels it. An interrupted connection cannot retain an old activation.</p><p>Other players can see your stone body. A brief original transformation chime and stone flourish mark activation, and STATUE appears above your status portrait. Separate sources of invisibility retain their own rules.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 45</div></article><article class="page" id="wand" data-title="A little borrowed sorcery"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little borrowed sorcery</h2><table><tr><td>Wand</td><td>LMB: piercing Beam · 12 charges · No reload</td></tr><tr><td>Wizard</td><td>Automatic class permission</td></tr><tr><td>Rogue</td><td>d100 ≤ 5 × Combat Level, capped at95</td></tr><tr><td>Fighter</td><td>May carry; cannot activate</td></tr></table><p>Each Wand is a distinct procedural weapon with its own twelve-charge pool. Equip a copy through Equipment and press LMB to attempt a Beam. One accepted attempt spends one charge and no personal Magic, with at least0.65 seconds between attempts. RMB still casts your selected Spellbook Magic.</p><p>Wizards activate Wands normally. Rogues make one non-exploding d100 roll:5% success at Level1,50% at Level10,95% at Levels19 and20. A failed Rogue attempt still consumes the charge and cooldown but produces no Beam. Other classes cannot activate it. Muted and Intimidated prevent activation without spending a charge.</p><p>A successful Wand Beam deals3d6 plus Wisdom through the shared Magic combat rules. It pierces eligible bodies once each in order and stops at blocking architecture or its normal range, extended by your existing spatial bonuses. Its cyan core keeps the familiar Beam identity; the Wand affinity supplies elemental damage and color accents.</p><p>The Wand uses its rolled equipment status chances and normal saves after effective damage; it does not add a second automatic Content rider. Your normal compatible damage modifiers and defenses still apply. Wand use does not unlock Beam or a Content in the Spellbook, and spending a charge does not count as spending personal Magic.</p><p>The remaining charges are always shown while the Wand is held and in its Equipment description. Empty Wands remain in your inventory. Reloading, ammunition pickups, regeneration, swapping copies, weapon recreation, respawning and dungeon transitions never refill a copy. Stored and frozen copies preserve their recorded charges. Attack attempts reveal Veil and end Statue.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 46</div></article><article class="page" id="death" data-title="Down, but not finished"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Down, but not finished</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>Heroes start with three lives. On death, your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions stay with your Hero for the respawn. Your character identity, XP, class, feats, and persistent DFT collection remain too. Reconnecting does not restore a lost life or reroll your character.</p><p>A normal death has a mandatory 20-second respawn lockout. Play Tetris while waiting, or spectate. After the lockout, press F to finalize your reward and respawn. You can keep playing until the normal 60-second death-Tetris maximum, when respawn happens automatically.</p><p>At zero lives, press F3 for three choices: wait for resurrection, begin a new Level-1 Hero, or join the Soldiers. Beginning again discards that character&#x27;s carried gear and progression but preserves account $DEB and DFTs. All Heroes may be eliminated without resetting the dungeon; only the collapse timer ends a failed run. Someone must still reach the rescue objective to clear it.</p><p>Successful rescue can bring eliminated teammates back under the campaign&#x27;s comeback rules, up to the maximum of four lives. A revived Hero returns with the same saved weapons, ammunition, wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 47</div></article><article class="page" id="tetris" data-title="Make room for a comeback"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make room for a comeback</h2><table><tr><td>One line</td><td>+10 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Two lines at once</td><td>+30 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Three lines at once</td><td>+50 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Four lines at once</td><td>+80 temporary HP</td></tr></table><p>Move pieces left or right, rotate with Up/Forward, soft-drop with Down/Back, and hard-drop with Jump. Clearing several lines at once earns the corresponding reward, not the sum of separate single-line clears.</p><p>Rewards become temporary HP overfill. They do not permanently increase maximum HP and cannot be refilled by ordinary regeneration. Topping out ends the board session while preserving the bonus already earned; it does not erase the mandatory death wait.</p><p>After rescue, Victory Tetris lasts 20 seconds. Russian Asset doubles qualifying HP rewards in both Tetris modes. It also raises the death-Tetris maximum to 120 seconds, but F still becomes available after the same 20-second lockout. It never extends Victory Tetris or the active dungeon clock.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 48</div></article><article class="page" id="soldiers" data-title="Friends—and human foes"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Friends—and human foes</h2><p>Up to four players take cooperative Hero roles. Up to six additional players can control enemy Soldiers, for ten active players; further arrivals spectate. Role availability and the Soldier queue determine when an incarnation can be entered.</p><p>A human Soldier receives a generated monster character, class, abilities, and automatically selected feats. The player does not draft Hero feats. A fresh incarnation is a fresh Soldier character, not the revival of the previous build.</p><p>Your Soldier carries a Pulse Rifle with INFINITE ammunition. Press primary fire once for a committed three-projectile burst after its visible warning; releasing does not cancel it, and holding does not start another. Extra Round, Extended Volley and Full Barrage still add their normal projectiles. No reload or ammunition supply is needed, even with an empty native magazine. Secondary input retains its existing Magic policy, never the stock energy orb. Ordinary Hero rifles still spend ammunition.</p><p>The burst keeps the direction aimed at commitment. Death, changing weapons, leaving the Soldier role, a replacement body, staging or a dungeon transition cancels unfinished shots; a server stall forfeits overdue shots rather than releasing a catch-up volley. The infinite permission belongs only to that Soldier incarnation and never transfers to your saved Hero. A committed burst roots your voluntary movement through its warning, projectiles and actual firing-rate-adjusted recovery. Releasing primary does not escape the commitment. You can still look and open F3; gravity, falls and forced knockback remain. Cancellation releases the root without transferring it to your saved Hero.</p><p>Human Soldiers walk at the ordinary AI Soldier base speed (140 units/second before existing modifiers), without ordinary sprinting or grounded jumps. Crouching, steps and stairs still work. Your generated class, DEX, status, Haste and directional movement effects retain their normal effects. An explicitly granted Wall Jump, Cloud Step or Float On remains usable while airborne outside a committed rifle attack; rooted attacks block these voluntary moves. Heroes keep their normal movement.</p><p>Human Soldiers can earn incarnation-local XP and automatic Combat Levels, capped by Dungeon Level +3. AI Soldiers do not earn XP. Monster-side Levels can reach 999. Soldier play preserves your eliminated Hero; return to the Hero Queue to become eligible for resurrection.</p><p>The Debbie Fund can reward Soldier-attributable Hero life losses after a successful rescue. Switching into Soldier play forfeits that player&#x27;s Hero share for that dungeon. The fund does not award a rescue pool for a timeout.</p><p>Press F3 at any time during ordinary Soldier play or the Soldier respawn wait to open the team menu. RETURN TO HERO QUEUE retires the Soldier and restores your saved Hero’s eligibility for future resurrection. It does not grant a life or bank revivals missed while playing a Soldier. SPECTATE ONLY retires the Soldier without entering the resurrection or automatic Hero queue. Press F3 again to return to the Hero queue.</p><p>Either exit discards that Soldier’s equipment and incarnation-local XP, not your saved Hero’s identity, progression, inventory, lives or original elimination order. A Soldier death requires twenty seconds before another Soldier incarnation; leaving its respawn wait cancels automatic respawning but does not skip that remaining delay. Your Hero can wait for resurrection independently.</p><p>Spectate-only preference survives reconnect and successful dungeon advance. Normal successful-clear life restoration still applies, but a spectator does not automatically become active. Returning with a life uses normal four-Hero admission and a fresh Hero spawn; a full team means waiting for a slot. A new campaign uses normal fresh admission.</p><p>The team menu never pauses combat or the dungeon clock. Finish any active Tetris or minigame first; campaign failure, expiry and level-transition locks still apply. Chat, console and text-entry focus do not activate F3. Hero feat choices and automatic Soldier progression are unchanged.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 49</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-basics" data-title="Meet the prison staff"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Meet the prison staff</h2><p>An enemy&#x27;s archetype tells you how it fights; its class, Combat Level, feats, and element make that particular creature different. Normal tier chances are 60% Typical, 30% Elite, and 10% Champion, using Dungeon Level, +1, and +2 respectively. A tier is a Level change, not a secret second set of damage multipliers.</p><p>Shamblers threaten close quarters; Runners close gaps quickly. Deadcrabs are low jumping attackers. Soldiers use ranged fire; Blitzers press aggressively; Snipers punish exposed sight lines.</p><p>BioBlasters threaten with biological attacks. Watchers demand attention to their readable attack tells. Seekers telegraph a committed charge: move out of its path instead of retreating along it.</p><p>Listen, look ahead, and use corners. Killing an enemy does not guarantee a permanently safe floor; wandering populations can replenish. Size, class tint, elemental aura, and aimed information help you decide what to tackle first.</p><p>Dungeons favor different enemy groups rather than showing the entire Bestiary at once. Expect frequent small patrol contacts and distinct tactical fights near the routes you explore, with brief breathing spaces rather than long empty sectors. The hunt before the Black Gate remains populated; Gordon’s arena stays reserved for its own encounter. Not every specialist can appear alone as a wanderer.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 50</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-new" data-title="More unwelcome company"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>More unwelcome company</h2><table><tr><td>Climber</td><td>Pink fast-zombie silhouette; climbs to reach you. Watch vertical approaches.</td></tr><tr><td>Nodule</td><td>Stationary gas hazard. Do not stand beside it.</td></tr><tr><td>Flamer</td><td>Red soldier silhouette; warns before a short-range fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Big Crab</td><td>Oversized headcrab with a telegraphed fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Sentry</td><td>Stationary gun emplacement; break its firing line.</td></tr><tr><td>Razor</td><td>Flying blade with a warned dive. Move off its committed approach.</td></tr><tr><td>Arc Caster</td><td>Marks a target area before its Magic strike. Leave the mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Lurker</td><td>Ceiling ambusher with a venom shot. Look up.</td></tr><tr><td>Beam Sweeper</td><td>Stationary sweeping laser. Interrupt its charge or use cover; gunfire will not cancel an already released beam.</td></tr></table><p>Fire can attempt Immolated; venom can attempt Poisoned. These enemies use the same saves, damage, elements, and progression rules as the rest of the roster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 51</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-control" data-title="The control detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The control detail</h2><table><tr><td>Gaoler</td><td>Icy-blue Vortigaunt. Its Ice attack marks a fixed patch of floor before erupting. Leave the mark or interrupt the charge: a failed Wisdom save can leave you Held while its Runner closes in.</td></tr><tr><td>Silencer</td><td>Pale-ivory Combine elite. A visible line warns of a straight, non-homing Light bolt. Sidestep or take cover. A failed Wisdom save can leave you Muted; guns and melee remain available.</td></tr><tr><td>Repulsor</td><td>Brown-and-gold Vortigaunt. It warns before an Earth pulse around its fixed casting position. Leave the ring or take cover. The hit can push you into a Soldier’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists appear with ordinary attackers. Identify the control threat early, then choose whether to interrupt it, evade its commitment, or remove its companion.</p><p>Held and Muted use the ordinary saves and recovery rules. Their warning colors identify the attack; a creature’s class and elemental affinity still belong to that individual. Cover and movement remain useful answers for every class.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 52</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-support" data-title="The prison support detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The prison support detail</h2><table><tr><td>Stitcher</td><td>A slave Vortigaunt channels recovery into one wounded ally. The green link announces a 1.5-second window to interrupt it or break its line of sight. Each completed channel restores 12% of the ally’s maximum HP, rounded up and capped at 24 HP; it cannot revive the dead.</td></tr><tr><td>Bulwark</td><td>A Combine elite protects one ally with a blue tether. Its six-second guard adds 25 percentage points to ordinary physical Block, still capped at 33%. Magic bypasses Block. Kill or interrupt the Bulwark, draw the ally more than 240 units away, or break the tether with cover.</td></tr><tr><td>Cantor</td><td>A Metrocop calls up to three nearby allies onto one visible Hero for four seconds. The gold call changes their target priority without adding damage or speed. Break sightlines, interrupt the caller, or turn the pursuing group into your team’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These enemies support existing companions; they neither summon reinforcements nor revive fallen enemies. Support does not stack. When nobody qualifies for support, each specialist can still fire a weak ordinary projectile.</p><p>Their distinctive links and call warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class and elemental affinity are independent of the support role.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 53</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-pursuit" data-title="Watch the other route"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch the other route</h2><table><tr><td>Pincer</td><td>A purple Combine soldier takes a legal alternate approach around the lane where it saw you, using a short loop or a two-leg flank within its own room. Its split chevron gives a brief warning before it moves. Watch the side approach, change lanes, or interrupt it; it cannot cross a locked gate or follow an unseen change of direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Harrier</td><td>A cyan Metrocop commits a visible firing line and releases one straight projectile before withdrawing. Its backward chevrons announce the retreat. Dodge the shot, then close the gap or deny its cover; interrupting the wind-up prevents the planned withdrawal.</td></tr><tr><td>Waylayer</td><td>An amber Combine elite marks a reachable junction with a square and cross. You have one second before it starts walking there. Take a different exit, interrupt it, or beat it to the junction. The marker deals no damage; its ordinary gunfire still warns before release.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists move through ordinary passages. Their plans are brief commitments to the position where they last saw a Hero, not knowledge of movement behind walls. Gates, obstructions, disabling conditions and interrupted plans can foil their maneuver.</p><p>Pincers appear beside Soldiers, Harriers beside Shamblers, and Waylayers beside Runners. Their route warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class, health and elemental affinity still belong to the individual.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 54</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-reactions" data-title="Make their response your opening"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make their response your opening</h2><table><tr><td>Pavise</td><td>The steel-blue Combine elite braces behind a shield-shaped tell. After a short warning, its guard stays aimed in one direction for 2.5 seconds. Frontal physical attacks face an extra Block chance, capped at 33%; it cannot walk or shoot while braced. Use Magic, circle to a flank, interrupt it, or wait for the guard to end. The ground arc shows the protected direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Repriser</td><td>The rose-colored Metrocop answers a direct damaging hit with a hooked-arrow warning and one straight projectile. Its one-second wind-up gives you time to sidestep, take cover or interrupt it with another damaging hit. It does not reflect your damage, and damage over time does not trigger the response.</td></tr><tr><td>Redliner</td><td>The rust-red Combine soldier normally fires from range. A direct hit that leaves it at 40% health or less can provoke one wounded rush: a jagged chevron announces the approach, followed by a one-second warning along its fixed lunge direction. Step aside or obstruct it. The lunge has three seconds of stationary recovery, shown by open bars; healing to 60% health rearms a later rush.</td></tr></table><p>Pavises appear with Runners, Reprisers with Soldiers, and Redliners with Shamblers. Watch both members of the pair: their different attack rhythms create different openings.</p><p>These responses keep ordinary class defenses, statuses and damage rules. Held stops the guard and lunge; Muted does not stop physical shots. Their warnings remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 55</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-patterns" data-title="Read the whole shot path"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the whole shot path</h2><table><tr><td>Caromer</td><td>The cyan Combine soldier draws a shot path with a diamond at its possible wall bank. After a 1.1-second warning, the physical projectile can ricochet once along the reflected line. Dodge the first lane and watch the second. New cover absorbs it; without a suitable wall it fires straight.</td></tr><tr><td>Reeler</td><td>The amber Metrocop warns along a fixed lane, then sends a shot beyond the position where it saw you. The shot pauses for three quarters of a second, marked by a hollow diamond, before returning to its original launch point. Sidestep, then stay clear until the return passes; cover stops either leg.</td></tr><tr><td>Forker</td><td>The mint-green Combine elite draws two parallel lanes for 1.2 seconds. Its paired shots leave the center open: hold that gap or move completely outside the lanes. There is no hidden middle shot. Cover and an interrupted wind-up can stop the volley.</td></tr></table><p>Caromers appear with Shamblers, Reelers with Runners, and Forkers with Soldiers. Companions can pressure the safe space, so read both attack rhythms. These are physical shots governed by ordinary class defenses and conditions.</p><p>Hit-stun can cancel the warning. A shot already in flight keeps its frozen path; it never follows a moving Hero. Each pattern can damage a given Hero at most once, including both Forker lanes. Reduced effects retain the path warnings.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 56</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-traps" data-title="Leave yourself an escape"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Leave yourself an escape</h2><table><tr><td>Wirewright</td><td>The cyan-and-orange Combine soldier marks a short tripwire across a lane. A hollow diamond and thin boundaries give 1.25 seconds of warning before it becomes live for five seconds. Endpoint posts and the upper rail show its jumpable height: jump above the wire, walk around either end, wait it out, or interrupt its maker. The narrow floor outline shows the full danger strip.</td></tr><tr><td>Snarer</td><td>The blue Vortigaunt arms a fixed circular snare after one second. It stays armed for at most six seconds. Entering with enough time left triggers one snap 1.25 seconds later, announced by inward teeth and a shrinking bar. Leave the circle to bait the snap safely; it cannot follow you or reset its timer. The Ice hit can leave you Held under the ordinary save rules. Mute, interrupt or defeat the Snarer to cancel it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cordon</td><td>The orange floor turret is a destructible pressure node. Two concentric rings warn for 1.5 seconds before one physical pulse, followed by a brief recovery. Enter the safe center, leave the outer ring, take cover, or interrupt or destroy the node to open the approach. The center stays safe; this is a single pulse, not lingering gas.</td></tr></table><p>Wirewrights appear with Runners, Snarers with Soldiers, and Cordons with Shamblers. Their companions pressure different escape routes; watch the whole encounter before committing.</p><p>These traps stay where they were marked. Thin boundaries and a hollow diamond mean arming; thicker boundaries mean live. Their finite warnings and escape routes remain visible with reduced effects.</p><p>A trap can hit a given Hero only once. Damage, defenses and conditions follow the ordinary combat rules. Interrupting or destroying the maker cancels its trap; removal never leaves a hidden hazard behind.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 57</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-melee-spacing" data-title="Respect the second swing"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Respect the second swing</h2><table><tr><td>Reaper</td><td>The green-tinted zombie marks a broad semicircle in front of itself for 1.1 seconds, then sweeps it once. Back beyond the arc, circle behind it, take cover or interrupt. Its facing stays fixed through the warning; exploit the 2.5-second stationary recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Drubber</td><td>The ochre fast zombie marks two nested, narrow attack sectors. After one second it strikes the inner sector; 0.85 seconds later it strikes the longer outer sector along the same fixed direction. Sidestep and wait for both beats. Rushing straight back after the first swing can put you into the second. It then recovers for 2.8 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Fencer</td><td>The pale-violet Metrocop shows a dashed retreat path and steps backward, then warns for 0.9 seconds before a long, narrow thrust from its marked endpoint. Resist chasing straight after it; move sideways out of the rectangle. Obstructing the retreat or interrupting the warning stops the attack. It recovers for 2.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Reapers appear with Soldiers, Drubbers with Runners, and Fencers with Shamblers. Their companions can contest the space you need to escape, so read the entire encounter.</p><p>All three make physical melee attacks under ordinary damage and defense rules. Interrupting them ends the commitment. Held stops a Fencer’s backstep but permits stationary strikes; Muted does not stop physical attacks. The fixed shapes and beat countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 58</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-remains" data-title="Watch what you leave behind"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch what you leave behind</h2><table><tr><td>Afterburst</td><td>The rust-orange zombie fights at close range. Defeating it leaves a fixed orange circle and shrinking bar around its corpse: after 0.8 seconds it bursts once. Back outside the circle or use cover before collecting its ordinary drop. Killing it stops its living attacks, but does not erase this warning.</td></tr><tr><td>Carrion</td><td>The green fast zombie can claim a nearby defeated ally when injured. A crossed-jaw mark, tether and shrinking bar announce 0.6 seconds of stationary feeding. Interrupt, kill or separate it, break the tether with cover, or defeat it before its Shambler escorts. A successful meal restores at most 30 HP, capped at maximum health. It gets one attempt per corpse and waits six seconds before another.</td></tr></table><p>An Afterburst appears with a Soldier; Carrion travels with Shamblers. Carrion can consume an Afterburst before it bursts, extinguishing the circle. Neither action creates another enemy or an extra reward; consumed remains still yield their ordinary drop on schedule.</p><p>Both enemies also warn a narrow, fixed melee sector. Sidestep or interrupt it. Held permits their stationary attacks and feeding; Muted does not stop these physical actions. Circles, tethers and countdowns remain readable with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 59</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-tether-cover" data-title="Break the line, cross the screen"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the line, cross the screen</h2><table><tr><td>Towline</td><td>The teal Metrocop marks a fixed narrow corridor and inward-pointing chevrons for 1.2 seconds. Sidestep, close inside 96 units, leave the lane or interrupt it. A successful physical hit can pull you a short distance toward it, subject to the ordinary push save. Blocked damage prevents the pull. Walls and missing floor support prevent movement. It then rests for three seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Screenwright</td><td>The blue Combine engineer warns a slatted screen for one second, then holds it for three seconds. This screen is freely passable. It adds 25 percentage points to ordinary physical Block for up to three allies behind it when your attack crosses the screen; the combined chance still caps at 33%. Flank, walk through, use Magic, interrupt the engineer or wait. The engineer itself receives no protection from its screen. Without suitable allies it fires an ordinary physical shot.</td></tr></table><p>Towline appears with a Runner; Screenwright appears with a Soldier. Read the whole encounter: the companions can exploit a pull or shelter behind the projected screen. No extra bodies or rewards are created.</p><p>Both channels remain stationary and interruptible. Held and Muted do not prohibit these physical actions. Their frozen shapes and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 60</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-mobile-hazards" data-title="Leave the route, mind the trail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Leave the route, mind the trail</h2><table><tr><td>Censer</td><td>The amber Combine soldier outlines its entire short route for 1.2 seconds, then carries a dangerous 64-unit circle along it. Step sideways out of the outlined lane, take cover, interrupt it or defeat it. Once movement begins, the thick ring follows its actual position. The carrier stops at its destination or after 1.8 seconds; it cannot steer after you.</td></tr><tr><td>Trailmaker</td><td>The green Metrocop warns a short retreat route for 1.2 seconds. Dashed circles show where it may leave up to three 44-unit patches as it reache]====]
